(Cell Biology Lecture: Cells, Membranes, and Energy) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Introduction) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cell biology is the study of the structure and function of cells. All living organisms are) Tj
T*
(made of one or more cells, and all cells arise from existing cells. This is known as cell) Tj
T*
(theory. Cells are the basic unit of life because they carry out the chemical reactions that) Tj
T*
(sustain life.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(There are two major categories of cells. Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, do not have a) Tj
T*
(membrane-bound nucleus. Their genetic material is located in a region called the nucleoid.) Tj
T*
(Eukaryotic cells, such as plant and animal cells, have a membrane-bound nucleus and) Tj
T*
(specialized compartments called organelles. Organelles allow different chemical reactions to) Tj
T*
(occur in separate environments.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The Plasma Membrane) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The plasma membrane is a selectively permeable barrier that surrounds every cell. It is made) Tj
T*
(mainly of a phospholipid bilayer. Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic phosphate head and two) Tj
T*
(hydrophobic fatty acid tails. In water, the tails face inward and the heads face outward,) Tj
T*
(forming a stable bilayer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Membrane proteins perform many functions. Channel proteins allow specific ions to pass) Tj
T*
(through the membrane. Carrier proteins change shape to move substances across the membrane.) Tj
T*
(Receptor proteins bind signaling molecules and trigger responses inside the cell. The fluid) Tj
T*
(mosaic model describes the membrane as a flexible mosaic of phospholipids, proteins,) Tj
T*
(cholesterol, and carbohydrates.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Passive transport moves substances from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower) Tj
T*
(concentration without using ATP. Diffusion and osmosis are examples of passive transport.) Tj
T*
(Active transport moves substances against their concentration gradient and requires energy,) Tj
T*
(usually from ATP. The sodium-potassium pump is an example of active transport: it moves) Tj
T*
(three sodium ions out of the cell and two potassium ions into the cell for each ATP that is) Tj
T*
(used.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
