(Organelles and Their Functions) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The nucleus contains most of the cell's DNA and controls gene expression. It is surrounded) Tj
T*
(by a double membrane called the nuclear envelope. The nucleolus, located inside the nucleus,) Tj
T*
(is the site of ribosome assembly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Ribosomes are the molecular machines that translate messenger RNA into proteins. They can be) Tj
T*
(free in the cytoplasm or attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum. The rough endoplasmic) Tj
T*
(reticulum is a network of membranes that helps fold and modify proteins. The smooth) Tj
T*
(endoplasmic reticulum makes lipids and helps detoxify harmful substances.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids for transport. It is) Tj
T*
(often described as the cell's post office. Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that break) Tj
T*
(down worn-out organelles, food particles, and invading bacteria. Vacuoles store water,) Tj
T*
(nutrients, and waste; plant cells usually have a large central vacuole that helps maintain) Tj
T*
(turgor pressure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mitochondria are the main site of aerobic respiration. They have an outer membrane and a) Tj
T*
(highly folded inner membrane called the cristae. The folds increase the surface area) Tj
T*
(available for the reactions that produce ATP. Mitochondria contain their own DNA and) Tj
T*
(ribosomes, which supports the endosymbiotic theory: mitochondria may have evolved from free-) Tj
T*
(living bacteria that were engulfed by an ancestral eukaryotic cell.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Chloroplasts are found in plant cells and some algae. They contain chlorophyll and carry out) Tj
T*
(photosynthesis, converting light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose. Both) Tj
T*
(mitochondria and chloroplasts have double membranes and their own DNA.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
