(Energy and Metabolism) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cells need energy to maintain order, move substances, build molecules, and reproduce. ATP,) Tj
T*
(or adenosine triphosphate, is the universal energy currency of the cell. ATP stores energy) Tj
T*
(in its phosphate bonds. When the terminal phosphate bond is broken, ATP becomes ADP and) Tj
T*
(inorganic phosphate, releasing energy that can be used for cellular work.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Cellular respiration is the process that converts glucose and oxygen into ATP, carbon) Tj
T*
(dioxide, and water. It has three main stages: glycolysis, the Krebs cycle, and the electron) Tj
T*
(transport chain. Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm and breaks one glucose molecule) Tj
T*
(into two pyruvate molecules, producing a small amount of ATP and NADH.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The Krebs cycle takes place in the mitochondrial matrix. It completes the oxidation of) Tj
T*
(pyruvate and produces NADH, FADH2, and a small amount of ATP. The electron transport chain) Tj
T*
(is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electrons from NADH and FADH2 pass through a) Tj
T*
(series of protein complexes, pumping protons into the intermembrane space. The protons then) Tj
T*
(flow back through ATP synthase, which produces most of the cell's ATP. Oxygen is the final) Tj
T*
(electron acceptor in this process.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Fermentation occurs when oxygen is not available. It allows glycolysis to continue by) Tj
T*
(regenerating NAD+. Lactic acid fermentation produces lactate, while alcoholic fermentation) Tj
T*
(produces ethanol and carbon dioxide. Fermentation produces much less ATP than aerobic) Tj
T*
(respiration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Photosynthesis is the reverse of cellular respiration in terms of energy flow. It uses light) Tj
T*
(energy, carbon dioxide, and water to produce glucose and oxygen. The light-dependent) Tj
T*
(reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes, and the Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma of the) Tj
T*
(chloroplast.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
